dels have been used as  pre-clinical models for many many years  and what's shown on this slide is the  classic xenograph model using a nude  mouse  so if you look on the left of this side  this is a nude mouse  that is harboring a tumor in this case  it's an ncf7 human breast tumor cell  line that's been injected into the mouse  under the skin and has grown into a  sizable tumor that you can see  now after treatment you see the tumor  can fully uh regress and disappear  the problem with these models are really  twofold first one is the need to implant  a br a cell line  these cell lines have been established  and grown in plastic for years sometimes  decades  and they really represent a pure cell  population that has little  relationship to the natural  heterogeneity of a tumor  in a human body where you have many  types of cells  basically that now in terms of the the  second issue with these this has to do  with the mouse itself nude mice are very  good at supporting uh these established  cell lines but their immune system is  such they typically will not support the  engraftment of more complex tissue such  as  real tumor tissue  and if we're going to study the  heterogeneity of real tumor tissue we  really need to be studying that tissue  itself not some purified cell line  now in order to come up with a mouse  that will support the engraftment of  human tissue you've got to step back and  look at the immune system that's what  this cartoon does for us  so when i think about the mouse model of  the and the immune system on the left  hand side we see the innate immune  system where we have the complement  system the macrophages and this is sort  of your first line of defense  um in the immune  system and then on the right side the  blue circles we have the adaptive immune  system things like t lymphocytes and  antibodies and the coordination of the  two systems through the cytokine  signaling  so this is a cartoon of an immune system  but when we're thinking abo