ut mouse  models you can also think of each of  these  uh circles as an obstacle to the  effective  graftman of human tissue and so what  we're going to do when we create a mouse  model that will accept human tumors is  we need to [ __ ] this immune system as  much as we can  and this is a project that's been going  on for years in our laboratory and many  others  but the latest state of the art is  something we call the nsg mouse and its  immune system is shown in this cartoon  where we've basically knocked out almost  all of the innate immune system except  for the granulocytes  we've knocked out almost the entire  adaptive immune system except for a  little bit of dendritic cell activity  and we've severely crippled the cytokine  signaling  so because the mouse is so immune  deficient it will accept a whole variety  of different human tissues including  solid tumors and liquid tumors like  leukemia and not reject those and so  this is the so-called nod skid mouse  which is the basis of all the pdx models  that i'll discuss today  as shown here the nod skid mouse or the  nsg mouse it's  deficient in t cells b cells natural  killer cells and very importantly it has  a long lifespan  uh as long as you maintain it in a  relatively sterile environment these  mice will live out easily past a year  and are very very healthy in fact and so  they do support the engraftment of a  whole variety of different tumors  and  and cell types so everything i'll talk  about today now  the all the pdx models are based upon  this sort of amazing nsg mouse  background  now when we are  creating these pdx models the most  important step is the first  that is getting the human tumor from the  clinician from the operating room and  getting it and grafted into a mouse  within 24 hours is our cut off and so  you can imagine the logistics there are  difficult and we've worked very hard  with a number of uh cancer centers  nationwide to put this network in place  where we can get the fresh tissue  directly from the operating suite  directly to our facility into the mouse  within 24 hours  now in our hands we find that just about  half of the tumors that we bring in  will engraft  successfully into the nsg mouse it  depends somewhat on the stage of the  tumor and how aggressive it is and also  the the type of tumor  for example certain types of breast  tumors require  um  estradiol for example and  you need to be able when grafting a  system that has the hormones needed but  in general we find about 50 percent as a  rule of thumb  now once those tubers have demonstrated  successfully and graft that triggers us  to do our characterization panel so we  do a variety of different genomic  characterization technique techniques  such as gene expression arrays we do  copy number variation analysis  we do a lot of  sequencing of specific genes to look for  mutations  and then we then we  place all those data into a database  we also then take that tumor we take  part of it and we cryobank it  but we take the other part and we expand  it so typically the tumor will be  then engrafted and what we call the p0  core heart shown here is for mice  typically it could be up to 10 mice or  we can graft that tumor and we grow up a  large enough volume or mass of that  tumor that we can then archive it  for future use  now these archive tumors then can be  used to create any number of different  mice very often we work with  pharmaceutical companies that are  testing drugs or combination of drugs  and there  will perhaps need 100 or 200 mice with  the same tumor in that so we grow those  from these donor mice  usually at the p3 or p4 level as passage  3 or passage 4. we can grow up to a  couple hundred mice and then those are  ready for any type of drug efficacy  studies  now in terms of the sources of these  tumors as i said we've established quite  a network uh i think early on we worked  a lot with uc davis because we are  located close to them but we've expanded  now into more than 20 different cancer  centers  these cancer centers are sending us  directly from the operating room both  liquid and solid tumors and along with  those we get a lot of the de-identified  patient identification such as the age  of the patient the treatment history etc  we also uh when we can we do receive the  histology  uh of it so we can compare the histology  of a patient tumor to the tumor once  it's been in the mouse  but typically in terms of experimental  work these are used for compound  efficacy studies that is testing single  agents or combination agents  in a very defined tumor with very  defined genetic character  characteristics  i will say that many of these studies  are are take place actually at our clia  lab in sacramento we have a variety of  surgeons and phd scientists that support  this  activity now where we are today this is  a little out of date but we have  somewhere between 350 and 400 of these  pdx models that we have validated  characterized  archived  and established for uh for use by the  research community you see it really  varies the type of tumors we have  bladder tumors brain tumors  a few endometrial tumors etc but uh our  goal here is to keep building and  working with the research community to  have a really comprehensive resource of  different tumor types with different  genetic characteristics  and all those data that we collect then  we make available  freely with free access to the research  community and this is one of the areas  that really sets the jackson laboratory  apart from other  people other  suppliers of pdx models is everything we  have we make a freely available to the  public in something called the  most the mouse tumor database this is  through the access to the jackson  website  and there's an area uh you can just  click on pdx tumor search  and you can search the entire database  by the tumor identifier the primary  tumor site the for example being a lung  tumor the diagnosis and very importantly  the gene variant so if you're looking  for a specific  mutation that a target a therapy is  targeted to  this database allows you to find  multiple models typically with a  specific genetic mutation for those drug  testing studies  so if i click on in this case a bladder  tumor example you see what comes up here  is an entire list of different tumors  that are available in the database with  patient information and also um some  information on the genetic makeup of the  tumor if i pick one of these  and click on it  and we dig down deeper and really see  what's available for each of these  tumors that is there's a patient  information  there are gene  uh  excuse me copy number variation gene  varied information growth characteristic  characteristics and all the genomic  information we have so again you can  just go publicly available website at  jackson laboratory and and review all  this information  uh also connected with each tumor we we  have what's shown here is the gene  expression it's called the z number  whether it's an over express or under  expresser of specific genes  and we also make available something  called copy number variation that looks  at the representation of specific genes  in the genome whether there are  more copies than normal or fewer copies  than normal  another aspect of our program here which  is quite unique is the way that  researchers can access these models  we provide and we're really one of the  only uh laboratories that provide what  are called study ready cohorts that is  these are mice that we have engrafted  with tumors characterized and we will  ship to any researcher that allow them  to do drug efficacy studies or other  basic biology studies so any of the  solid tumors we have in the tumor  database  we have some aml models that are very  popular that's acute myeloid leukemia  these are engrafted with  myelo leukemia cells that are isolated  from individual patients and are used to  develop anti-leukemia drugs  and we also have a very popular language  called humanized nsg mice these are mice  that have been engrafted with human  hematopoietic stem cells and develop  human immune systems for infectious  disease research the sort of a sister  product line  in addition to providing these  study-ready cohorts to investigators we  also can do drug efficacy testing at our  clia facility here in sacramento and we  do  quite a volume of business working with  biotech and pharmaceutical companies and  some academics as well to test new drugs  and we have a new program that's shown  here the third thing called pdx live  and this is a way of taking the most  popular tumors and making them available  to uh researchers quickly one of the  traditional problems with pdx and models  is it takes quite a while to take a  a  tumor from the freezer thought and  grafted and grew up a cohort it can take  several months to generate the mice  needed for a study and with pdx live  what we've done is taken the most  popular tumors  some for example some of the breast  colon and lung tumors that are very  widely used and we maintain those mice  on the shelf and so very often you can  get the cohorts of mice within a week  days weeks or  maybe at most a few weeks not having to  wait for the months that it  traditionally took  now in terms of areas where we're strong  i think one of the big areas is a  non-small cell lung cancer what was  shown here is a panel of the non-small  cell lung cancer models that we  routinely work with and make available  and you see we have a lot of information  about the patient background  and the genetic makeup of the tumor and  the genetic makeup of the of the pdx and  basically most of the driver genetic  mutations that are of interest in  research are available from our  resource  we've also taken pains to be  deep in this area of triple negative  breast cancer when i say triple negative  that means is estrogen receptor negative  progesterone receptor negative and her2  new receptor negative  and this is important clinically because  these tumors  are troublesome to treat typically have  to be attacked with combination therapy  and our very active area of research  for new therapeutics and so  we have  established this panel here we're going  through a lot of the standard of care  drug studies on these now and looking to  expand this even further  now i mentioned the the estrogen  receptor negative  breast cancers on the last slide what's  shown here are a couple of receptor  positive  tumors on the left we see an estrogen  receptor positive  tumor and on right a progesterone  receptor positive tumor  the importance here is these estrogen  receptor example positive tumors  typically would not grow in mouse models  because they're dependent on human  estrogen for their growth  and  a lot of the previous mouse  models would not tolerate  the presence of the human estrogen they  would develop crystals in their bladder  etc now we found that the human  the that the human excuse me the nsg  mouse is very good at tolerating uh the  presence of estrogen and in fact we  typically implant a small capsule of  estrogen  in the neck the neck of the of the mouse  and it does support the growth of these  estrogen receptor positive tumors a very  um  sort of unique  niche for this model  i want to talk now about the structural  fidelity of these tumors because it's  really quite fascinating  and remarkable i think what's shown here  are some histology slides from a colon  adenocarcinoma  and this is a k-ras mutation a very  common driver gene  in colon other types of  carcinoma  on the left  slide or photo you see the patient tumor  histology  uh in the middle you see the p0 or first  passage of the tumor through a mouse  histology and secondly the p1 the second  passage of the tumor and what's  remarkable you'll see here are these  crypt-like structures that are very um  indicative of the adenocarcinoma type of  histology so you see that these uh  structures developed with remarkable  fidelity uh at multiple passages in  these pdx models  also shown here are two histology  sections of a colon adenocarcinoma from  a metastatic growth that is taken from  the lung and we see on the left this is  the patient histology  on the right we see after the first  passage through a mouse and again the  characteristic glandular structures that  you'd expect to see in an adenocarcinoma  are very faithfully recapitulated in the  mouse model  sticking with adenocarcinoma this is an  example  of a non-small cell lung adenocarcinoma  and the point here is really of  to focus on the gene expression data  we see the histology here of the  patient tumor the passage zero the  passage one and the passage two with the  recapitulation of again the glandular  type of histology  but this tumor  gene expression data shows first that  it's a k-ras mutant so k-ras is probably  a driver gene in this and it's shown to  be egfr wild type  and the importance there is there are a  variety of different uh therapeutic  agents that target mutant egfr  and so if we look at this tumor  our patient we'd expect for those  uh therapeutics that target mutant egfr  not to work  things like erlotinib and in fact when  we test this tumor that's exactly what  we see  what's shown here in the black line is  the vehicle control  and this is a growth curve  what's shown in the blue line is that  same tumor treated with aerolotinib  which targets the the mutant egfr we see  essentially no impact of that drug  because this tumor does not have the  mutation  but when treated with cysts platinum is  a red line we see it does respond so  the point here is these pdx tumors have  been shown to be  really remarkably good at predicting  therapeutic outcome and which drugs will  work and which drugs won't at recent  conferences i've been to  the number is typically that for the  older models such as the nude mice with  cell lines the protective power is only  about 30 percent  so it's not that much better than random  sometimes but these pdx models are often  are seeing greater than 90  uh predictive power  for the the use of drugs and this is  really what's gotten the oncology  community very very excited about these  models  the other very exciting thing about  these models is what you can do with  them in terms of studying different  drugs  you can really evaluate everything  that's available  by creating enough mice and designing  the right study things you could never  even dream of doing in a human setting  so you can rank order the efficacy of  single agents  very exciting you can study different  drug combinations  not only drug combinations but the  timing that is drug a first followed by  drug b  or maybe you'll try but drug b for  first followed by drug a and this let  you let you really kind of probe into  the onset of resistance and how the  drugs can interplay so uh really a  fascinating tool for studying the  interplay between different drug and  dosing regimens for individual tumors  i'm going to change gears now and talk  about liquid tumors and this is an area  where the nsg host has been a real  superstar so very often you cannot these  liquid  things like leukemias have not taken  very good hold in different mouse models  they really work very well with the nsg  host  and in these models what we do is we  engraft these mice with leukopharesis  samples so these are samples of cells  that are isolated from individual  leukemia patients  preserved and then injected into mice to  create these models  and the two models that we work most  commonly with one is called an uh flit  or flt3 itd  this is  a  it's basically a growth factor receptor  uh and it's muta this itd this internal  tanning repeat is one of the most common  if not the most common mutation seen in  aml patients  secondly is something called an idh1  mutant idh1 and idh2  they're actually  metabolic enzymes and when they're  mutated like this  they  interfere with the normal  differentiation of cells and so  the two models i'll talk about today and  what we most commonly sell  are the flip 3itd models and the idh1  mutant models  to create these is actually a quite a a  simple process we take the mice  we do a whole body irradiation which  create let's sort of opens up a niche in  the cells in them  opens up a niche in the um bone marrow  of the mice we can then inject these  human leukemia cells they will take hold  in the  bone marrow and other places  about 12 or she's about 14 to 28 weeks  and as we monitor those we see the human  aml cells appearing in the peripheral  blood and in other organs as well  this is an example here of  where we find these  these populations of the human  aml cells so on the left side we see two  different models  uh in terms of where the  the percentage of cells in the blood at  different time points we see the cells  in the spleen at different time points  and in the bone marrow so  you really do see these cells  proliferate as you'd expect a leukemia  to do in a human setting and we also see  these models responding to standard of  care drugs exactly like you'd expect a  human aml patient to respond in this  case the drug is called era c it's a  standard of care for many amls and we'll  see the decrease on the left here of the  number of aml cells in the bone marrow  of these models  we see a decrease in these cells in the  spleen  these models and also in the peripheral  blood so again therapeutically  the cells in the mouse model respond  exactly as you'd expect the cells to  respond in a human model  now i'm going to conclude now by talking  about sort of where the future of this  work is going  and  uh it really has to do with  excuse me again um  the human immune system and how the  human immune system is going to be can  be modulated  i think the most uh  active area of cancer research right  right now is human immuno modulatory  therapy  where you  modulate the human immune system to  attack the tumor itself  and well i'll talk about now conclude  are different models  that we are moving toward  uh for modeling  human immunotherapy  and that begins with something we call  the the humanized nsg or in this case  the cd 34 plus humanize nsg what we're  going to do here is create a mouse  that has the human immune system  as a first step  so again we create these models exactly  as we created the human aml models that  is by irradiation of the mouse to create  a niche in the bone marrow  in this case we're going to engraft  human hematophytic stem cells  and then out after several weeks we see  the appearance of human b cells human t  cells and most of the component of human  immune cells that you would typically  see  there are several different models of  this  that display different levels of human  immune function the most common is the  so-called cd34 humanized mice these are  created as i just said by engraftment of  human hematopoietic stem cells  these have a full range of human immune  cells uh with some exceptions but the  big thing here they have a very long  healthy lifespan many months  and uh so it can be used for very long  studies  the downside of these they do have  somewhat weak b cell function  and poor igg function so if you're in a  system where you need uh you need that b  cell function or more igg production  you might consider something that's  called the blt humanized mouse which is  based on the cd34  but has  the difference being we do implant small  pieces of liver  and thymus under the kidney capsule of  these mice and what that does it helps  with the t cell maturation and we do get  improved immune function in these cells  for certain things like mucosal immunity  if you're studying hiv infection in the  body you don't see any coastal immunity  in the cd34 cells you do definitely see  it in the blt mice  the downside of having the more souped  up immune system though is the mice do  not live as long these typically last  about 20 weeks and they develop a graft  versus host type of of  phenotype so improved immune system  life span a little bit shorter  also available or something called pbmc  humanized mice we basically take the nsg  mouse and just engraft the full  complement of pbmcs from a human  directly into the mouse  the upside of these is they do have the  full complement of human immune cells  but again you do have the short life  span and after about 30 days those cells  then will turn around the mouse and you  get this graph  versus also so depending on the type of  what you're studying  there are a variety of different options  here  and all these options are being looked  at today in the very early stages  to see if these can be models for  immunotherapy in the future that is  uh you know they're used for infectious  diseases shown on this slide they're  used for to study gene therapy and et  cetera but they can really be used to  study cancer we're hoping  because  if you can take an individual mouse and  grafted both with human immune system  cells and with human tumor cells i think  it's going to open some fantastic areas  for research  and this is just my concluding slide  today uh showing that that what  the areas where these humanized mice are  are really moving forward and the third  point is what i want to talk about these  human immuno-oncology models  is really our focus moving forward and i  think you'll see the field move very  aggressively to these new type of  pdx models very soon now  so i want to thank you for joining me i  hope i provided a good overview of the  field of pdx work and i'd be very happy  to take any questions now submitted  through your chat window  oh and i do have some now i figured out  the first question has to do with pdx  live uh it says how long will these  tumor models  uh maintain  their original traits  for on the shelf i think it basically  has to do with the stability of these  models  with the pdx live  what 